@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-requirements Requirements
@brief Requirements are checks that stop the test case when they fail.

@details
`t.require (condition)` checks a condition as `expect()` does, and also
stops the test case when it fails. Use it when the rest of the test case
depends on the condition, for example to check that a pointer is not null
before using it.

A failed requirement is reported and counted like a failed expectation.
What follows depends on the build:
- with exceptions, the rest of the test case is skipped, and the run
  continues with the next test case; this is the behaviour of the fatal
  assertions of most C++ test frameworks (`ASSERT_*` in GoogleTest,
  `REQUIRE()` in Catch2 and doctest, `BOOST_REQUIRE()` in Boost.Test);
- without exceptions, a test case cannot be stopped alone, so the whole
  test run is aborted with `runner::abort()`, as Catch2 does: the results
  of the test case are written, ending with the failed requirement
  (followed, with the TAP reporter, by a `Bail out!` line), the remaining
  test cases do not run, and no totals are written. The process is then
  terminated with `::abort()`, unless the application set another handler
  with `runner::set_abort_handler()`.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check the buffer", [] (auto& t) {
  const char* buffer = get_buffer ();
  t.require (mt::ne (buffer, nullptr)) << "buffer allocated";
  t.expect (mt::eq (buffer[0], 'a'));
});
@endcode
